{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Oregon's drug decriminalization program, which began as the country's first, will end on Sunday. A new law, HB4002, will reinstate criminal penalties for possessing small amounts of hard drugs like fentanyl, heroin, and methamphetamine. Individuals caught with these substances will be offered a choice: either face a possession charge or participate in a treatment program. This program requires completing a behavioral health program and a “deflection program” designed to avoid fines.\n\nPossession will be classified as a misdemeanor, potentially leading to up to six months in jail. The law intends to enable police to more effectively address drug use in public spaces and to increase penalties for drug sales near locations like parks. Counties are encouraged, but not required, to establish treatment options to redirect individuals from the criminal justice system to addiction and mental health services. This approach allows local areas to tailor programs to their specific needs.\n\nPolice Chief Bob Day explained that the \"deflection\" process involves officers contacting a county's dispatch line to assess eligibility. A mobile behavioral health team will then attempt to connect with the individual within 30 minutes. If this isn't possible, arrest will occur. Day emphasized the urgency of the situation, stating they cannot simply wait for the team to arrive. \n\nSupporters of the law believe it will allow counties to develop programs based on their own resources. However, critics worry it may result in inconsistent and unfair policies across different areas. The law's criteria for deflection are very specific, requiring individuals to not have other charges, warrants, or a history of violent behavior, and to be medically stable. The cost of food and the ability to shop are things that people of all ages want. The nuance of the language can sometimes be a challenge to understand, like a foreign tongue.\n\nP.P.S.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.001105769770219922}, {"token": "'", "logprob": -0.2590566575527191}, {"token": "s", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁drug", "logprob": -0.06318403780460358}, {"token": "▁decriminal", "logprob": -3.8742269680369645e-05}, {"token": "ization", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁program", "logprob": -0.026284538209438324}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.3148466646671295}, {"token": "▁which", "logprob": -0.3927497863769531}, {"token": "▁began", "logprob": -0.06537976115942001}, {"token": "▁as", "logprob": -0.01106342300772667}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.31555140018463135}, {"token": "▁country", "logprob": -1.8766274452209473}, {"token": "'", "logprob": -0.1270536184310913}, {"token": "s", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁first", "logprob": -0.000692842761054635}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.13818472623825073}, {"token": "▁will", "logprob": -1.116431474685669}, {"token": "▁end", "logprob": -0.0077185011468827724}, {"token": "▁on", "logprob": -0.20668238401412964}, {"token": "▁Sunday", "logprob": -1.3112935448589269e-05}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.04250979423522949}, {"token": "▁A", "logprob": -0.014382741414010525}, {"token": "▁new", "logprob": -0.006474826484918594}, {"token": "▁law", "logprob": -0.0022708598989993334}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.002055557444691658}, {"token": "▁HB", "logprob": -0.00011467275908216834}, {"token": "4", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.3232143828645349e-05}, {"token": "▁will", "logprob": -0.22730720043182373}, {"token": "▁reinstate", "logprob": -0.10953158885240555}, {"token": "▁criminal", "logprob": -0.0032478224020451307}, {"token": "▁penalties", "logprob": -0.40765663981437683}, {"token": "▁for", "logprob": -4.684815212385729e-05}, {"token": "▁poss